      "stageRecord": "Merge matrix T and raise the complex v head to it. T agrees with first-person singular pro and values nominative Case; pro then moves to the matrix subject position, with both occurrences remaining silent. The highest verbal occurrence pronounces Quiero as a whole present-indicative first-person singular word. Merge a silent declarative root C to complete the sentence. The final pronounced sequence is Quiero que Ana venga mañana, but mañana is attached to the matrix wanting predicate rather than the embedded coming predicate. This reading locates the wanting tomorrow and requires a prospective use of the present; it does not itself specify tomorrow as the time of Ana’s arrival. Head and subject movements retain silent copies, and the overt complementizer introduces a finite subjunctive clause rather than an infinitival control complement.",
